mtk_eth_soc: fix warm reset on MT7988 (poll for deasserted FE, not asserted)

The nixos delta's mtk_hw_warm_reset() asserts RSTCTRL_FE and polls for the
asserted bit to read back set. On MT7988 the FE clears the reset condition
asynchronously, so the readback is already clear while the write is in
flight: every poll times out, every recovery attempt falls into the cold
ethsys-reset fallback, and the MACs stay down (no link re-train) until the
next cold boot.

Observed on s-nodus (BPI-R4 Pro 4E): "warm reset failed" twice in 22s
followed by total network outage (WAN DHCP dead) until reboot.

Fix per the OpenWrt mtk_eth_soc form: assert, settle, deassert, then poll
for the deasserted state to be visible, with a 100ms timeout. Keep the cold
reset fallback for the genuinely wedged case, with an explicit log line.
diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c
index 504468de8..51f670630 100644
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c
@@ -4376,9 +4376,12 @@ static void mtk_hw_warm_reset(struct mtk_eth *eth)
 
 	regmap_update_bits(eth->ethsys, ETHSYS_RSTCTRL, RSTCTRL_FE,
 			   RSTCTRL_FE);
+	udelay(1);
+	regmap_update_bits(eth->ethsys, ETHSYS_RSTCTRL, RSTCTRL_FE, 0);
 	if (readx_poll_timeout_atomic(mtk_hw_reset_read, eth, val,
-				      val & RSTCTRL_FE, 1, 1000)) {
-		dev_err(eth->dev, "warm reset failed\n");
+				      !(val & RSTCTRL_FE), 1, 100000)) {
+		dev_err(eth->dev,
+			"warm reset failed; falling back to cold ethsys reset\n");
 		mtk_hw_reset(eth);
 		return;
 	}
